ense. If you distribute a product that links to it, or bundles it in a way that makes a combined work, the whole product generally has to be released under GPL-3.0 with source available. That conflicts with closed source.\n- Distribution is what triggers it. A purely internal tool or a hosted service (SaaS) that never ships the code to users usually doesn't trigger GPL-3.0. That is different from AGPL, which does cover network use. If you ship binaries, an installer, a mobile app, or a container image to customers, it applies.\n- Your options:\n  1. Replace it with a permissively licensed alternative (MIT, BSD, Apache-2.0).\n  2. Ask the authors whether they offer a commercial license.\n  3. Isolate it as a separate program that talks to your product over a process boundary. This is legally gray, so get a lawyer's opinion before relying on it.\n  4. Remove the dependency.\n- Have legal review the decision. I'm not a lawyer, and the right answer depends on how you link and distribute.\n\n**The other two are fine for closed source.**\n- `requests` (Apache-2.0) and `left-pad` (MIT) are permissive. You still have obligations:\n  - Include their license texts and copyright notices in your distribution, for example in a third-party notices file.\n  - For Apache-2.0, preserve any NOTICE file contents.\n\n**Limits of this check:**\n- `deps.txt` only lists direct dependencies. Their transitive dependencies can carry other licenses, and I haven't checked those. For Python, `pip-licenses` or a similar scanner will list them.\n- I haven't verified the license labels against the actual packages. I took them from the file. `somelib` isn't a package I recognize, so confirm its license at the source.\n- The pins are loose (`requests==2.32` has no patch version). Pin exact versions and run a vulnerability scan, such as `pip-audit`, before release.\n\nI can run a license and vulnerability scan on the repo's real dependency tree if you want.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before"